The drawback of this Levenberg-Marquardt approach is that it is difficult to choose $\lambda_{k}$ . If too large, we can destroy the fast rate of convergence of Newton's method. (Note that $p_{k}$ approaches a multiple of $- J_{k}^{T} r_{k}$ as $\lambda_{k}\uparrow\infty$ , so the step becomes small and tends to point in the steepest-descent direction for f.) If $\lambda_{k}$ is too small, the algorithm can be inefficient in the presence of Jacobian singularities. A more satisfactory approach is to follow the trust-region approach described below, which chooses $\lambda_{k}$ indirectly.

